se 4:\nQuery Rewrite Approach 1: Use a single query with JOIN and GROUP BY\nThe original query uses two separate queries to get the data. This can be rewritten into a single query that joins the tables and groups the results.\nQuery Rewrite Approach 2: Use a subquery in the FROM clause\nThe original query has a subquery in the FROM clause. This can be rewritten as a subquery in the FROM clause, which may be faster.\n\nCase 5:\nQuery Rewrite Approach 1: Use a single query with JOIN and GROUP BY\nThe original query uses two separate queries to get the data. This can be rewritten into a single query that joins the tables and groups the results.\nQuery Rewrite Approach 2: Use a subquery in the FROM clause\nThe original query has a subquery in the FROM clause. This can be rewritten as a subquery in the FROM clause, which may be faster.\n\nNote: The above is just an example of how to rewrite the queries. The actual query rewrite approaches and explanations will depend on the specific queries and requirements.', 'time': 6.245200872421265}
